   "text": "The drawing shows a methyl group bonded to selenium, then a CH2, then a stereocentre carrying NH2 and a carboxylic acid (C=O with OH). This is Se-methylselenocysteine, CH3\u2013Se\u2013CH2\u2013CH(NH2)\u2013COOH.\n\nThe NH2 is on a hashed bond, so it points away from the viewer, and the implicit hydrogen points toward the viewer.\n\nCIP priorities at the stereocentre are:\n1. N\n2. CH2Se (Se outranks O)\n3. COOH\n4. H\n\nViewed from the front, N (bottom) \u2192 CH2Se (upper right) \u2192 COOH (upper left) runs counterclockwise. Because H points toward the viewer, the sense is reversed, so the centre is R. This is the L-configuration, as in L-cysteine.\n\nFor the SMILES, I compared against L-cysteine, N[C@@H](CS)C(=O)O. Writing the neighbours in the order CH2, H, N, COOH swaps N and CH2 relative to that string, which flips the chirality tag. The result is C[Se]C[C@H](N)C(=O)O, which is the R/L form.\n\nSMILES: C[Se]C[C@H](N)C(=O)O"
